2. Precision - Too carefull may say NO
Precision provides the accuracy of the positive prediction made by the classifier. 
** use precision when cost of  FP is high ( spam detection, where important emails should not be missed0
Precision = True Positive /  (True Positive + False Positive)
** A model with high precision is very good at avoiding mistakes when it says “yes.” 


3. Recall - Too deligient and may say Yes 
Recall is the ratio of number of true positive predictions (correctly detected by the classifer) to the t
otal number of actual positive instances in the dataset. It measures the completeness of positive predictions. 
** Use recall when cost of FN is high ( cancer detection where you cannot miss a psotive case)
Recall = True Positve / (True Positive + False Negative)
** A model with high recall is very good at not missing anything important. It finds almost all the actual “yes” cases in 
the data


4. F1 Score-
F1 Score is invaluable in binary classification tasks, especially with imbalanced datasets, 
where accuracy can be misleading. It strikes a balance between precision and recall, crucial in scenarios where both 
are equally important, like medical diagnosis. This metric effectively captures the trade-off between precision and recall, 
offering a comprehensive evaluation of model performance.

f1 = 2* (precision X recall)/ (presicion + recall)

The score ranges from 0 to 1, with 1 being a perfect model (perfect precision and recall) and 0 being the worst.
Range Interpretation: Generally,
>0.9 is excellent
0.7–0.9 is good
0.5–0.7 is okay
 <0.5 is poor. 

